.TH SECURITY 7
.SH NAME
security \- who may do what in SIEOS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS Identity
The kernel gives every process an identity when it starts: a user number
(uid), a group number (gid) and up to 16 more groups.
It never changes afterwards.
The kernel stamps the sender's pid, uid and gid on every message, so a
server always knows who asks and can trust it; the other groups it asks with
.BR sys_ident (2).
uid 0 is
.BR root ,
the administrator.
.PP
Only root may start a program under another identity
.RB ( sys_spawn (2)
with a uid, gid and groups); every other program's children get their
parent's identity.
There is nothing like a program that gains rights when run: a user who
needs something done that their identity does not allow asks a server that
is allowed, and that server decides.
.SS The supervisor and drivers
The first process,
.BR init (8),
is the supervisor: it alone may give device rights (I/O ports, interrupts,
device memory, DMA:
.BR SPAWN_DRIVER ),
start boot modules, and declare servers started on demand.
Only drivers may map device memory (never RAM), bind interrupts, allocate
DMA memory or reach PCI configuration space.
A dead driver's DMA buffers stay unused for 100 ms before they are reused.
.SS Root only
Powering off, restarting and suspending
.RB ( sys_power (2)),
setting the clock, starting programs as another user, signalling other
users' processes (no one may signal the supervisor), changing a file's owner,
making volumes.
A process may signal its own processes;
.BR sys_note (2)
reaches root's or the same user's.
.SS Files
The file server
.RB ( fs (4))
checks every access with the caller's uid, gid and groups against the
owner, group and the usual rwx bits; root may do anything.
.B /tmp
is sticky: a user may not delete other people's files there.
Homes are mode 0700 and each is a volume of its own,
.BR encrypted :
its key is wrapped by a key derived from the user's password (Argon2id), so
on the disk nothing of it can be read without the password.
It opens at a login with the password, and stays open until the machine
stops: what it protects is the disk at rest, not a running system, where
root can read everything anyway.
.SS Passwords
Only the accounts server,
.BR auth (8),
reads the password hashes
.RB ( users (5)).
A wrong password costs one more second; an unknown name costs the same time
as a known one; comparisons take the same time wherever the bytes differ;
passwords are erased from memory after use.
.SS Memory
Programs' own files are mapped W^X: no page is both writable and
executable (anonymous memory may be, for a JIT's code pool).
A process may get a memory quota (SPAWN_QUOTA); a child never gets more than
its parent's.
A shared memory object is named by a random 63-bit number: knowing it is the
right to use it.
.SS Remote logins
.BR sshd (8)
checks passwords through auth, as login does; root may log in with a key
only; 6 tries per connection, 2 minutes to log in.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR users (5),
.BR auth (8),
.BR fs (4),
.BR ipc (7),
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR sys_kill (2)
